the world health organization's estimate  of close to 15 million global deaths due  to covet 19 is nearly three times the  official death count of just under five  and a half million as reported by  governments  the who says it used statistical  modeling to arrive at the expanded  number which includes deaths directly  from covid and from other medical  conditions that went untreated due to  the pandemic strain on health systems  this is extremely important information  for anyone to have especially policy  makers and the public we need to know  the true toll of the pandemic we have to  remember that behind these numbers are  people according to the who's report  covet-related deaths in india were  nearly 10 times higher than the official  count indian health officials pushed  back against the report thursday  attacking the who's use of statistical  modeling please appreciate that these  kind of assumptions to be used for a  nation of our size of a continent size  and to put us in poor light is not  desirable other countries with large  discrepancies include pakistan where  according to the w-h-o excess deaths  were eight times higher than the  official kovid death count  in egypt it was 12 times higher  and in mexico it was double i think um  the experts all along uh were very aware  of the uh let's say shortcomings or  tools that were not available right from  the beginning so  um the amount of testing etc would mean  that there were many deaths that went  unaccounted for  the wh says most of the excess deaths  were concentrated in southeast asia  europe and the americas and were in  middle income countries they also  confirmed the global death toll was  higher for men who accounted for 57  percent of deaths  the who is the first to caution that its  report on global coveted related deaths  is an estimate and thus imperfect but  the agency says it is nevertheless  important to get an accurate accounting  of the pandemic's toll to better prepare  for future threats  heidi joe castro al jazeera washington
 

